\par 1. The Storage: Registers
\par Before anything can happen, the CPU pulls data from your RAM and places it into Registers.
\par 
\par What they do: They hold the specific numbers you want to add or compare.
\par 
\par The "Where": These are located directly inside the CPU for maximum speed.
\par 
\par 2. The Muscle: The ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)
\par This is where the actual operations happen.
\par 
\par What it does: The ALU is the calculator of the CPU. Once the numbers are in the Registers, the ALU "grabs" them, performs the operation (like addition, subtraction, or logic like AND/OR), and spits out a result.
\par 
\par The "Where": The ALU is a physical circuit right next to the registers.
\par 
\par 3. The Boss: The Control Unit (CU)
\par This is where the execution is managed.
\par Instruction Sequencing (The Schedule)
\par Fetching and Decoding (The Translation)
\par Data Routing (The Traffic Control)
\par Clock Signal Management (The Heartbeat)
\par The CU synchronizes everything using the internal system clock.
\par 5. Control Signals (The Command)
\par Once the CU knows what needs to happen, it sends out specific "Control Signals" to other components:
\par 
\par Read/Write Signals: Tells the RAM whether to send data out or save data coming in.
\par 
\par ALU Signals: Tells the ALU which physical circuit to activate (e.g., "Use the Addition circuit, not the Subtraction one").
\par 
\par It sends out electrical pulses that tell every other part of the CPU: "Okay, MOVE NOW." This prevents data from colliding or getting lost in the "tunnels" of the circuitry.
