\par 1. Filtering (The "Sieve")
\par These methods look through your array and only keep the elements that meet a condition.
\par 
\par Where(): Filters based on a condition (e.g., arr.Where(x => x > 10)).
\par 
\par OfType<T>(): Filters elements based on their ability to be cast to a specific type.
\par 
\par Distinct(): Removes all duplicate values.
\par 
\par 2. Projection (The "Transformer")
\par These change the shape or type of your data.
\par 
\par Select(): Transforms each element (e.g., arr.Select(x => x * 2) doubles every number).
\par 
\par SelectMany(): Flattens "arrays within arrays" into one single linear list.
\par 
\par 3. Ordering (The "Organizer")
\par OrderBy(): Sorts in ascending order.
\par 
\par OrderByDescending(): Sorts in descending order.
\par 
\par ThenBy() / ThenByDescending(): Used after a primary sort to break "ties" (e.g., sort by Last Name, then by First Name).
\par 
\par 4. Aggregation (The "Calculator")
\par These turn your entire array into a single number or value.
\par 
\par Sum(): Adds everything up.
\par 
\par Min() / Max(): Finds the smallest or largest value.
\par 
\par Average(): Calculates the mean.
\par 
\par Count(): Returns the total number of elements.
\par 
\par Aggregate(): A custom "accumulator" (e.g., multiplying all numbers together).
\par 
\par 5. Quantifiers (The "Checkers")
\par These return a bool (true/false).
\par 
\par Any(): Returns true if at least one element matches a condition.
\par 
\par All(): Returns true only if every single element matches.
\par 
\par Contains(): Checks if a specific value exists in the array.
\par 
\par 6. Element Operators (The "Pickers")
\par These grab one specific item from the list.
\par 
\par First() / FirstOrDefault(): Grabs the very first item.
\par 
\par Last() / LastOrDefault(): Grabs the very last item.
\par 
\par ElementAt(index): Grabs the item at a specific position (similar to arr[i]).
\par 
\par Single(): Returns the only element in a list (throws an error if there\rquote s more than one).
\par 
\par 7. Set Operations (The "Comparers")
\par These are what we used for your Union/Intersection question.
\par 
\par Union(): Combines two arrays and removes duplicates.
\par 
\par Intersect(): Finds elements common to both arrays.
\par 
\par Except(): Finds elements in the first array that are not in the second.
\par 
\par Concat(): Simply stitches two arrays together (keeps duplicates).
\par 
\par 8. Partitioning (The "Slicers")
\par Take(n): Grabs the first n elements and ignores the rest.
